+# Multimodal Meme Media, Muse, Gesture, Architecture, and Translation Runtime
+
+## Purpose
+
+Allow memes and folklore to inhabit sound, movement, objects, tools, clothing, buildings, vehicles, machine behavior, and nonhuman sensory forms rather than reducing future culture to captioned rectangles.
+
+> **The world should sometimes recognize a joke before the player knows how to read it.**
+
+# 1. Media Families
+
+Supported artifact families:
+
+```text
+text
+still image
+short moving image
+voice clip
+musical motif
+rhythm or chant
+gesture
+pose
+movement sequence
+machine diagnostic behavior
+clothing or body presentation
+object modification
+tool ritual
+architecture
+spatial arrangement
+light pattern
+haptic pattern
+chemical or scent pattern
+pressure or fluid pattern
+hybrid multisensory form
+```
+
+Each medium declares:
+
+- production requirements;
+- creator skill;
+- storage and transmission cost;
+- accessibility alternatives;
+- reproduction fidelity;
+- mutation affordances;
+- audience sensory prerequisites;
+- privacy risk;
+- persistence.
+
+# 2. Media Object
+
+```rust
+struct MemeticMediaObject {
+    media_id: MediaId,
+    variant_id: MemeVariantId,
+    modality: Modality,
+    source_assets: Vec<AssetRef>,
+    generated_assets: Vec<AssetRef>,
+    creator_operations: Vec<CreativeOperation>,
+    duration: Option<Duration>,
+    spatial_extent: Option<SpatialBounds>,
+    sensory_requirements: Vec<SensoryRequirement>,
+    accessibility_alternatives: Vec<AccessibleRepresentation>,
+    rights: RightsState,
+    integrity_hash: Hash,
+}
+```
+
+Media generation occurs only after a valid creator action or culturally simulated production event.
+
+# 3. Muse Integration
+
+Muse supplies musical and rhythmic expression while remaining subordinate to cultural and worldline truth.
+
+Inputs include:
+
+- source event rhythm;
+- associated environmental or machine sounds;
+- creator culture and style knowledge;
+- desired social function;
+- audience familiarity;
+- instrument access;
+- meter and motif fatigue;
+- emotional tone;
+- duration and performance context;
+- copyright and provenance constraints.
+
+Muse may produce:
+
+- a two-note reaction sting;
+- a work chant;
+- a parody motif;
+- a vehicle horn pattern;
+- a festival call-and-response;
+- a machine timing joke;
+- a longer song that incorporates a meme;
+- variants adapted by later cultures.
+
+Muse must not:
+
+- quote protected real-world music without authorization;
+- assign one musical stereotype to an entire culture;
+- make every meme catchy;
+- force emotional interpretation through soundtrack;
+- reveal a private source event;
+- overwrite an authored local song.
+
+# 4. Motif Genome
+
+A memetic motif stores:
+
+```text
+interval contour
+rhythmic cell
+accent structure
+timbre family
+source-sound reference
+call-and-response role
+variation permissions
+cultural style bindings
+associated phrase or gesture
+```
+
+A motif may mutate through:
+
+- instrumentation;
+- tempo;
+- mode;
+- rhythm displacement;
+- truncation;
+- inversion;
+- translation into machine timing;
+- embedding in another song;
+- deliberate wrong performance.
+
+The system preserves motif lineage separately from whole compositions.
+
+# 5. Environmental Sampling
+
+Memes may derive from world sounds:
+
+- pump cavitation;
+- crawler suspension noise;
+- Atlas synchronization tones;
+- a machine startup sequence;
+- alien pressure pulses;
+- a politician’s vocal cadence;
+- a door failure;
+- a festival generator;
+- a bird imitating an alarm.
+
+Sampling requires:
+
+- an observer or recording device;
+- access to the source;
+- legal and privacy state;
+- transform record;
+- attribution where culturally expected.
+
+# 6. Gesture and Pose Memes
+
+Gestures are represented as semantic movement graphs rather than fixed animation clips only.
+
+```rust
+struct GestureMeme {
+    gesture_id: GestureId,
+    variant_id: MemeVariantId,
+    body_requirements: BodyConstraintSet,
+    motion_primitives: Vec<MotionPrimitive>,
+    timing_profile: TimingProfile,
+    gaze_and_orientation: Vec<OrientationCue>,
+    social_contexts: Vec<ContextId>,
+    prohibited_contexts: Vec<ContextId>,
+    accessible_variants: Vec<GestureVariantId>,
+}
+```
+
+Examples:
+
+- a Vast heroic pose used after trivial success;
+- a transit-worker shrug imitating crawler suspension;
+- a machine pause meaning “you ignored the warning again”;
+- a child’s route-game salute;
+- an alien current pattern reproduced by human dancers.
+
+A gesture must adapt to different bodies without treating one body as the canonical complete version.
+
+# 7. Architectural Memes
+
+Buildings and spaces may carry jokes through:
+
+- deliberately oversized doors after a famous Vast incident;
+- drainage channels shaped like an old official diagram;
+- seats oriented toward the place a founder never arrived;
+- a tiny ceremonial arch attached to an enormous industrial hall;
+- route stations repeating a famous timing error in floor patterns;
+- crawler modules preserving a useless Firstlight lamp as a “navigation beacon.”
+
+Architectural memes require:
+
+- labor;
+- materials;
+- permission or trespass;
+- maintenance;
+- visibility;
+- inhabitants who recognize or reinterpret them.
+
+They can become landmarks after their joke is forgotten.
+
+# 8. Object and Tool Memes
+
+Objects may become memetic through:
+
+- repeated labels;
+- inherited damage;
+- decorative repair;
+- ceremonial misuse;
+- famous provenance;
+- copied form;
+- workplace ritual.
+
+The physical object remains authoritative. A meme does not generate an item without fabrication or modification.
+
+Examples:
+
+- a wrench labeled “temporary governance”;
+- the chair saved during Firstlight reproduced as a convoy emblem;
+- a failed filter mounted above a maintenance desk;
+- an Atlas clock with an intentionally late decorative hand;
+- a machine’s obsolete component worn as a badge.
+
+# 9. Clothing and Body Presentation
+
+Memetic fashion may include:
+
+- prints;
+- pose silhouettes;
+- color combinations;
+- wearable tool replicas;
+- body-light patterns;
+- hair or chassis styling;
+- deliberately exaggerated protection gear;
+- anti-brand alterations.
+
+Adoption depends on body compatibility, culture, cost, climate, safety, and social meaning.
+
+The runtime must avoid converting sacred or restricted body practices into generic cosmetics without source-community participation.
+
+# 10. Machine Media
+
+Machine humor may use:
+
+- timing;
+- process order;
+- diagnostic text;
+- actuator motion;
+- sensor masking;
+- network response codes;
+- deliberate resource inefficiency;
+- state synchronization.
+
+Some machine memes are not perceptible to humans without translation.
+
+A human-accessibility layer may describe:
+
+- relative delay;
+- deliberate deviation;
+- repeated pattern;
+- social context.
+
+It should not always translate the exact punchline, because partial incomprehension may be culturally meaningful.
+
+# 11. Alien Media
+
+Nonhuman media packages declare:
+
+- sensory channels;
+- safe human exposure ranges;
+- temporal scale;
+- spatial scale;
+- agency structure;
+- translation confidence;
+- whether observation changes the artifact;
+- whether performance requires living participants.
+
+Examples:
+
+## Oceanic
+
+Pressure and current fields whose “caption” is the route taken by suspended material.
+
+## Lithic
+
+A shadow alignment built over years.
+
+## Symbiotic
+
+Several organisms exchanging roles during a repeated task.
+
+## Post-Biological
+
+Forked performances where each copy edits one timing layer.
+
+## Quarantine
+
+One-way message forms that imply impossible replies.
+
+# 12. Translation Runtime
+
+Translation produces a `CulturalTranslationRecord`:
+
+```text
+source artifact
+source meaning estimates
+target audience
+perceptual conversion
+language conversion
+lost distinctions
+added associations
+safety adaptation
+confidence
+translator identities
+```
+
+Possible outcomes:
+
+- direct recognition;
+- explanatory translation;
+- approximate emotional equivalent;
+- new hybrid meme;
+- failed translation;
+- accidental offense;
+- false sacred interpretation;
+- deliberate refusal to translate.
+
+No universal translator should make all humor immediately transparent.
+
+# 13. Accessibility
+
+Every surfaced meme must declare whether its meaning depends on:
+
+- sound;
+- pitch;
+- rhythm;
+- color;
+- facial expression;
+- motion;
+- haptics;
+- spatial position;
+- reading speed;
+- language register;
+- sensory patterns outside ordinary human range.
+
+Alternatives may include:
+
+- descriptive text;
+- rhythm visualization;
+- haptic substitution;
+- simplified gesture;
+- high-contrast variant;
+- transcript;
+- contextual explanation on request;
+- IRIS uncertainty note.
+
+The alternative should preserve the structure of contrast or timing when possible.
+
+Do not require understanding optional humor for critical safety, consent, or progression information.
+
+# 14. Production Budgets
+
+Initial proof target:
+
+- 20 textual frames;
+- 12 visual compositions;
+- 10 short audio and motif frames;
+- 8 gestures;
+- 6 object or tool transformations;
+- 4 architectural forms;
+- 4 machine-only forms;
+- 3 alien translation demonstrations;
+- accessibility alternatives for every surfaced artifact.
+
+Only a small number should appear per session.
+
+# 15. Caching and Determinism
+
+Generated media stores:
+
+- source asset hashes;
+- operation graph;
+- generation model and version;
+- random seed;
+- output hash;
+- accessibility representations;
+- rights state.
+
+Authoritative multiplayer distributes verified outputs or deterministic operation graphs.
+
+# 16. Safety and Quality Gates
+
+Reject media that:
+
+- alters a person’s body or voice beyond consent;
+- creates intimate or sexualized content without permission;
+- makes a patient or child identifiable;
+- uses inaccessible flashing or audio patterns without safety controls;
+- contains unsupported text in another language;
+- misrepresents a protected cultural form;
+- generates impossible object provenance;
+- violates body physics for a supposedly embodied gesture;
+- depends on a copyrighted external artifact without authorization;
+- is merely visual noise with a caption.
+
+# 17. Test Scenarios
+
+1. crawler suspension sound becomes a youth dance;
+2. same rhythm becomes a maintenance warning and must remain distinguishable;
+3. a Vast pose adapts to several bodies;
+4. a private voice clip cannot be sampled publicly;
+5. an IRIS timing joke receives caption and haptic alternatives;
+6. a tool label persists after the source phrase is forgotten;
+7. an architectural joke consumes real labor and survives demographic change;
+8. an alien pressure form produces three translation interpretations;
+9. Muse creates two culturally distinct variants from one source event;
+10. an attempted corporate remix fails cultural selection;
+11. multiplayer clients verify identical media hashes;
+12. reduced-meme settings preserve world state while limiting presentation.
+
+## Runtime Maxim
+
+> **The future’s funniest joke may be a sound in an engine, a building placed slightly wrong, a gesture performed by three bodies, or a song whose original words disappeared a century ago.**
